<section>
  <div class="section-head">
    <div class="kicker">Season Three</div>
    <h2>The Clues &amp; The Reveals</h2>
  </div>


  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E27 → S4E3 · Clues given December 22, 2024 · Revealed January 19, 2025</div>
    <h3>Spokane, Washington</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Incorporated in the 1880s; started as a trading post; name from a native language</li>
      <li>"Fathers should really thank this city" (birthplace of Father's Day)</li>
      <li>The world spotlight in 1974 (Expo '74 World's Fair)</li>
      <li>A former resident shaped our perfect Christmas (Bing Crosby grew up there)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Spokane, WA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Franklin Johnson's wrong "Spokane" guess for Seneca inspired Michael to make it the
    next city. Only guess: Adam — "something north" (technically the upper 50%).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E26 → S3E27 · Clues given December 8 · Revealed December 22, 2024</div>
    <h3>Seneca, South Carolina</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded 1870s; named for a Native American village; built at its location for the railroad</li>
      <li>Mills drove the economy for 70+ years; surrounded by three lakes</li>
      <li>Three major fires in its first 40 years</li>
      <li>A main drag that "did not get its name from a stand-up routine, but it could have" (Ram Cat Alley)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Seneca, SC</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Franklin Johnson — Spokane, WA, awarded a sixth of a point for getting
    the first letter right.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E25 → S3E26 · Clues given November 24 · Revealed December 8, 2024</div>
    <h3>Burlington, Ontario</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Land purchased by the British from the native inhabitants</li>
      <li>Annually celebrates its founder — not a US citizen (Mohawk Chief Joseph Brant)</li>
      <li>Not incorporated until 1914; named for a nearby geological feature (a local bay)</li>
      <li>Watch where you park, or you might end up asking "what's up?" (the King's Road gravity hill)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Burlington, Ontario</span></div>
    <p class="winners">The first Canadian spotlight. Facebook bonus hint: the Great White North album cover
    — all Ontarians, Geddy Lee included. No correct guesses; Stephen would only say "Canadian money is
    where my mouth is."</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E24 → S3E25 · Clues given November 10 · Revealed November 24, 2024</div>
    <h3>Sacramento, California</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>The original name was Swiss in origin (New Helvetia)</li>
      <li>If you're a squirrel or a bird, this is the town for you (scratched at the reveal)</li>
      <li>One of the oldest incorporated communities in its state; first big industry was gold</li>
      <li>A major role in early communications (Pony Express terminus; the transcontinental telegraph)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Sacramento, CA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Credit: Matt Robinson — Placerville, CA, ~50 miles off (and called "Mark" again).
    Late full answers from a private-text friend and Stephen Witts, Bushmills 16 on standby.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E23 → S3E24 · Clues given October 27 · Revealed November 10, 2024</div>
    <h3>Pagosa Springs, Colorado</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Mid-1850s settlers came for the mineral springs ("let's not let details get in the way of a good story")</li>
      <li>Known for a large amount of recreational opportunities</li>
      <li>In the late '50s, a serious science student would have wanted to attend this town's high school</li>
      <li>Drive carefully, or you might end up in the side of the feed store</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Pagosa Springs, CO</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Matt Robinson — Huntsville, AL (NASA-plus-springs logic). Same episode,
    Michael unveiled his own spotlight page on the CMOL website.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E22 → S3E23 · Clues given October 13 · Revealed October 27, 2024 — the Halloween episode</div>
    <h3>Columbus, Ohio</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded February 14, 1812 at the confluence of two rivers</li>
      <li>Named for a European explorer with no actual connection to the city — no one knows why</li>
      <li>Originally French; ceded to Britain by the Treaty of Paris (1783)</li>
      <li>Instrumental in the labor organization movement; a notable Heisman Trophy connection</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Columbus, OH</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Adam texted Michael a confident secret guess after recording: St. Louis. Wrong twice
    over — a Michigan man bested by Ohio.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E21 → S3E22 · Clues given September 29 · Revealed October 13, 2024 — the Maria Parker episode</div>
    <h3>Dubuque, Iowa</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Named after a fur trader granted mining rights by the Spanish government and local native people</li>
      <li>You can see three states from a spot in town; the settlement predates the state itself</li>
      <li>Post–Civil War economy of transportation and lumber</li>
      <li>Pioneered TV reception (1954 — among the first US towns wired with coaxial cable)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Dubuque, IA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Matt Robinson — Soda Springs, ID, admitting he'd hit his ChatGPT
    question limit.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E20 → S3E21 · Clues given September 15 — the 50th episode · Revealed September 29, 2024</div>
    <h3>Duluth, Minnesota</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A harbor with a unique distinction (biggest inlet harbor in the world; largest US freshwater port)</li>
      <li>Influenced how large numbers of people travel (Greyhound began in the area)</li>
      <li>Once held a very fiery title (Match Capital of the World)</li>
      <li>Local tales suggest you might need a bigger boat (the disputed first freshwater shark attack)</li>
      <li>A little credit for a major award in literature (Bob Dylan — 2016 Nobel Prize)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Duluth, MN</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guesses: Adam — an impulsive Boston, then "West Coast" via Jaws logic (Jaws is
    set in New England); and cousin Mark — San Pedro, CA.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E19 → S3E20 · Clues given September 1 · Revealed September 15, 2024 — on the 50th</div>
    <h3>Waxhaw, North Carolina</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Began as a Native American settlement; named for the indigenous Waxhaw people</li>
      <li>German and Scotch-Irish farmers; textile mills; the railroad became the center of town</li>
      <li>A hundred-plus-year-old bridge over the tracks downtown</li>
      <li>A connection to US recognition of the Republic of Texas (Andrew Jackson, born in the Waxhaws)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Waxhaw, NC</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Ed — the 1.6×-speed listener — with double gold stars, because it's his
    own town. Adam's on-air guess: Dallas, TX.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E18 → S3E19 · Clues given August 18 · Revealed September 1, 2024</div>
    <h3>Beltsville, Maryland</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Started as a land grant (1649, from Lord Baltimore); named for landowner Truman Belt</li>
      <li>Muirkirk furnace iron supplied US Army cannons in the Revolutionary and Civil Wars</li>
      <li>Home of Brig. Gen. Rezin Beall — why Maryland has no Revolutionary War battlefields</li>
      <li>Bugs live in fear (inventor Lyle Goodhue, of aerosol "bug bomb" fame)</li>
      <li>Its own cryptid — the Goatman, blamed for canine deaths since the early '70s</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Beltsville, MD</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No correct guesses — the cryptid clue sent Adam thinking "foresty areas" and no further.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E17 → S3E18 · Clues given August 4 · Revealed August 18, 2024</div>
    <h3>Missoula, Montana</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Started as the Hellgate Trading Post (1866)</li>
      <li>In a state originally part of another state's territory (Washington Territory, until 1863)</li>
      <li>A front line for firefighting (the Missoula Smokejumper Base)</li>
      <li>A craft-beer destination (fourth in the US per capita)</li>
      <li>World-renowned for a large rock (the claimed largest gold nugget ever found — $19,000 in 1889)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Missoula, MT</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Steven — reasoning from Lewis &amp; Clark to "what became of the
    acquisition." Adam guessed Denver ("there's a big rock there") despite having lived there 13 years.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E16 → S3E17 · Clues given July 21 · Revealed August 4, 2024 — recorded in person before Shoreline</div>
    <h3>Sioux City, Iowa</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Laid out in the mid-1800s; named in honor of the Sioux</li>
      <li>Promoted America's love of candy (Palmer Candy Co., 1878 — the oldest in the US)</li>
      <li>Known for what an expedition left behind (Lewis &amp; Clark's Sgt. Charles Floyd Jr., the
      expedition's only death, buried on a bluff under a monument)</li>
      <li>Fundamental to a beloved cartoon character (Art Babbitt, who shaped Disney's Goofy)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Sioux City, IA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guess: Adam — Albuquerque, NM. Bonus fact: George Koval, the only Soviet agent
    inside the Manhattan Project, was also from Sioux City.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E15 → S3E16 · Clues given July 7 · Revealed July 21, 2024</div>
    <h3>Paris, Texas</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Most famously known for a well-recognized landmark (an Eiffel Tower replica — with a giant cowboy hat)</li>
      <li>A county named for a VP who became president (Lamar County — Mirabeau Lamar of the Republic of Texas)</li>
      <li>A huge railroad center</li>
      <li>A connection to the 1889 Paris World's Fair (Edison, Tesla, Buffalo Bill, and Annie Oakley attended)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Paris, TX</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Wrong guesses: Bonnie Goldberg of the Super Stragglers — Kansas City, MO; Steven —
    Denver, CO, via a full Paris Exposition analysis that earned style points anyway.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E14 → S3E15 · Clues given June 23 — the BRAG wrap-up · Revealed July 7, 2024</div>
    <h3>Haymarket, Virginia</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Formally founded in the 1790s; early growth from district-court commerce</li>
      <li>Burned in the Civil War — only four buildings survived</li>
      <li>Mickey Mouse &amp; Co. had a vested interest (the cancelled early-'90s "Disney's America" park)</li>
      <li>Over 38 golf courses within 20 miles</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Haymarket, VA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No guesses — though the golf clue had Adam packing his bags and pricing timeshares.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E12 → S3E13 · Clues given May 26 · Revealed June 16, 2024 — BRAG Part One</div>
    <h3>Seattle, Washington</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>First Europeans visited in 1792; a history of boom and bust</li>
      <li>You could say it's the smartest city in the US; very bike friendly</li>
      <li>Its most famous landmark was basically doodled on a napkin (the Space Needle)</li>
      <li>Hometown of someone who taught fans that two Winchesters can take out almost anything
      (Supernatural's Jeffrey Dean Morgan)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Seattle, WA</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Steven Witts — first tempted by Vancouver, Canada, until he remembered
    the clues said an American town.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E11 → S3E12 · Clues given May 12 · Revealed May 26, 2024</div>
    <h3>Owensboro, Kentucky</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>First settled 1797; originally Yellow Banks, for the color of the land on the Ohio</li>
      <li>Renamed in 1817 for Col. Abraham Owens, killed at Tippecanoe — the very battle William Henry
      Harrison rode to the presidency</li>
      <li>Tobacco stemmeries, distilleries (Glenmore is still there), and wagon companies</li>
      <li>A bluegrass hotspot (the Bluegrass Hall of Fame and Museum) — and Johnny Depp's hometown</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Owensboro, KY</span></div>
    <p class="winners">🏆 Winner: Johnny Lampkin — his own hometown, answered backward with receipts.
    The FIRST listener ever to solve a spotlight.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E10 → S3E11 · Clues given April 28 · Revealed May 12, 2024</div>
    <h3>Reno, Nevada</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A definite stop for professional cowboys (the Reno Rodeo)</li>
      <li>Birthplace of a piece of worldwide fashion (riveted Levi's blue jeans)</li>
      <li>Named for a Civil War general (Jesse L. Reno, killed at South Mountain, 1862)</li>
      <li>On a westward-expansion trail (the California Trail)</li>
      <li>Gilligan's Island owes it some success (Dawn Wells — Mary Ann — was from Reno)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Reno, NV</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No guesses — but the reveal ran long on Mary Ann appreciation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E9 → S3E10 · Clues given April 14 · Revealed April 28, 2024</div>
    <h3>Westbrook, Maine</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Originally part of another town — renamed twice</li>
      <li>Finally named for Col. Thomas Westbrook of the New England militia</li>
      <li>A mill town on the Presumpscot River</li>
      <li>In a state it wasn't originally established in (Maine was Massachusetts until 1820)</li>
      <li>A canal ran through it from 1832 (the Cumberland &amp; Oxford, linking the lakes to Portland)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Westbrook, ME</span></div>
    <p class="winners">No guesses — Adam wandered the Kentucky-Tennessee-Illinois tri-corner and never got out.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E8 → S3E9 · Clues given March 31 · Revealed April 14, 2024</div>
    <h3>Tucson, Arizona</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded as a military fort by the Spanish</li>
      <li>A giant in the temporary tattoo industry (the world's largest maker)</li>
      <li>A notorious gangster was captured there (John Dillinger)</li>
      <li>Well known in US cycling (the Tucson Bicycling Classic)</li>
      <li>Ansel Adams played a significant role (co-founding the U of A's Center for Creative Photography)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Tucson, AZ</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Adam's trail of thought went straight to Puerto Rico and stalled there.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E7 → S3E8 · Clues given March 17 · Revealed March 31, 2024</div>
    <h3>Dublin, Ireland</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>A town heavily associated with beer — home to over 750 drinking establishments</li>
      <li>Maybe a couple of witch burnings back in the old days</li>
      <li>An association with a famous lion (the original MGM lion — Adam actually called that clue)</li>
      <li>The name translates to something concerning water</li>
      <li>One of the largest parks in the region (Phoenix Park)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Dublin, Ireland</span></div>
    <p class="winners">The segment's first international city. Adam's official guess: St. Louis. His own
    review: "How far away could you be?"</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E6 → S3E7 · Clues given March 3 · Revealed March 17, 2024 — St. Patrick's Day</div>
    <h3>Starkville, Mississippi</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Settlers drawn by two large springs; created 1834</li>
      <li>Originally "Boardtown," for its clapboard mill</li>
      <li>Renamed for Revolutionary War hero Gen. John Stark</li>
      <li>Lindbergh landed on the outskirts in 1927; Johnny Cash was arrested there in 1965 — inspiring
      "Starkville City Jail"</li>
      <li>One of several claimed birthplaces of tee-ball</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Starkville, MS</span></div>
    <p class="winners">Adam went 0-for-3: Pittsburgh and St. Louis by text, Louisville, KY on air. A
    listener 15 miles away wrote in delighted afterward.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E5 → S3E6 · Clues given February 18 · Revealed March 3, 2024 — the first clue mystery</div>
    <h3>Lewisville, Texas</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
      <li>Founded in the 1840s; home to the county's first cotton gin</li>
      <li>A major lake completed in 1950 (Lewisville Lake, outside Dallas)</li>
      <li>Not its original name — a gentleman bought the town and renamed it after himself</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="answer"><span class="label">Answer</span><span class="city">Lewisville, TX</span></div>
    <p class="winners">The guessing game began here — Michael: "You've got to play a little Sherlock
    Holmes." Adam's guess: "somewhere in Georgia" (cotton gin logic).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ep">
    <div class="meta">S3E4 · February 4, 2024 — the Listener Spotlight is born</div>
    <h3>Durango, Iowa &amp; Big Lake, Minnesota</h3>
    <ul class="clues">
